The South China Sea (SCS) is not a distant problem for the Republic of China (ROC). It is an extension of our home waters, the maritime space that connects Taiwan to Southeast Asia. For a mid-sized democracy living next to a rising authoritarian neighbor, strategy cannot be improvised. It must be built on a clear logic that links what we want, how we will get it, and what we will spend getting there. This essay uses a realism framework to sketch a grounded ROC strategy for the SCS, informed by Carl von Clausewitz.

Realism is the appropriate lens for Taipei. Stephen Walt reminds us that realism "depicts international affairs as a struggle for power among self-interested states" and that it "remains the most compelling general framework for understanding international relations" (Walt, 1998, 31). For an island that the People’s Republic of China (PRC) openly threatens with force, wishful thinking about shared norms is a luxury we cannot afford. Power, balance, and deterrence must come first.

Clausewitz anchors the military side of the strategy. His insistence that "war is merely the continuation of policy by other means" reminds the ROC that any use of force in the SCS must serve political ends (Clausewitz, 1976, 87). The Taiwanese strategy therefore must be politically disciplined and aimed at the adversary’s center of gravity. In the PRC case, that is the legitimacy of the Chinese Communist Party tied to uninterrupted economic growth and a narrative of inevitable unification.

The ROC’s ends in the SCS flow directly from its survival as a self-governing democracy. Yarger notes that strategic ends are "specific to the issue of the strategy" and must be stated clearly enough to guide action (Yarger, 2006, 6). Four ends are proposed.

First, preserve ROC sovereignty over Taiping and Pratas and the surrounding exclusive economic zones. These outposts give Taipei a legal voice in any multilateral settlement and a forward sensor line that extends our maritime domain awareness several hundred nautical miles south.

Second, keep the sea lines of communication through the SCS open, since roughly half of our energy imports and a large share of our trade transit these waters. Closing the SCS is, for Taiwan, equivalent to slow strangulation.

Third, deny the PRC a cheap fait accompli. The strategic goal is not to defeat the People’s Liberation Army (PLA) in a blue water battle. It is to raise the political and military cost of coercion high enough that Beijing judges the attempt not worth the risk. This echoes Clausewitz’s observation that the defender’s task is to make the aggressor’s object "not seem worth the effort" (Clausewitz, 1976, 93).

Fourth, deepen functional partnerships with the United States, Japan, the Philippines, Vietnam, and Australia without triggering a premature crisis. Walt’s realism captures the logic exactly when he writes that states "will join alliances to protect themselves from states or coalitions whose superior resources could pose a threat" (Walt, 1998, 37).

The ways describe how Taiwan converts these ends into effect. Five interlocking lines of effort are proposed to achieve these ends.

The first way is layered deterrence by denial. Drawing on Clausewitz, the ROC should treat its forward islands as tripwires rather than fortresses, backed by a dense mainland centered anti-access complex of mobile missiles, sea mines, unmanned surface vehicles, and hardened command posts. The idea is not to win a symmetrical war but to ensure, as Clausewitz puts it, that "the defensive form of warfare is intrinsically stronger than the offensive" when used to protect a political position (Clausewitz, 1976, 84).

The second way is diplomatic hedging under strategic ambiguity. Taipei should continue to claim historic rights under the 1947 eleven dash line in principle, while in practice aligning its SCS conduct with the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS). This gives us legal room to cooperate with Manila, Hanoi, and Kuala Lumpur without formally abandoning our historical position. Realism, Walt notes, accepts that "states are rational actors" who weigh costs and benefits.  Flexible legal posture is a rational way to lower the political price of cooperation (Walt, 1998, 31).

The third way is coast guard led presence. The ROC Coast Guard Administration, rather than the Navy, should be the main face of our SCS presence. Gray hulls invite escalation, while white hulls normalize daily administration. Regular patrols, search and rescue exercises, and scientific missions around Taiping and Pratas reinforce occupation without handing Beijing a propaganda victory.

The fourth way is economic and informational statecraft. Taipei should fund marine scientific research at Taiping, publish open data on fisheries and environmental conditions, and host regional academics. This reframes the ROC as a responsible maritime steward rather than a claimant to be ignored. It also complicates the PRC’s narrative that only Beijing speaks for "China" in the SCS.

The fifth way is quiet interoperability with partners. Through unofficial channels and second track dialogues, the ROC can align procurement, communications, and rules of engagement with the United States and Japan. Clausewitz’s warning that "no one starts a war, or rather, no one in his senses ought to do so, without first being clear in his mind what he intends to achieve" applies here to our partners as well, and interoperability is the practical expression of shared political intent (Clausewitz, 1976, 579).

Means are the resources we can realistically commit. Yarger cautions that "means are the resources available to pursue the objectives," and that a strategy which outruns its means is no strategy at all (Yarger, 2006, 7).

On the military side, the ROC should continue building force, with emphasis on coastal defense cruise missiles, mobile air defenses, naval mines, small missile boats, and long endurance drones. Large surface combatants are symbolically attractive but poor value given PLA missile density. A modest logistics upgrade at Taiping, including runway resilience and fuel storage, increases the political cost of any seizure attempt without being openly provocative.

On the civilian and paramilitary side, the Coast Guard needs more offshore patrol vessels, unmanned aerial systems, and a dedicated SCS squadron rotating through Pratas. Fishery subsidies and insurance should encourage Taiwanese vessels to keep operating in the area, since an empty sea is easier to annex than a busy one.

On the diplomatic and economic side, means include targeted development assistance to Southeast Asian partners under the New Southbound Policy, semiconductor supply chain leverage, and participation in Track 1.5 forums. These instruments are modest in dollar terms but disproportionate in effect, because they tie our partners’ prosperity to our continued autonomy.

On the informational side, means include a standing interagency cell that monitors PRC gray zone activity, publishes attributable reports in English, Mandarin, and Southeast Asian languages, and coordinates with partner think tanks. Narrative is cheap compared to ships, and under a realist reading of power it still matters because it shapes third party behavior.

The main risk of this strategy is entrapment. If Taipei leans too far forward in the SCS, Beijing may use a Spratly incident as a pretext for action against Taiwan proper. Clausewitz’s concept of friction, the idea that "everything in war is very simple, but the simplest thing is difficult," is a reminder that even a well calibrated patrol can escalate through accident or misperception (Clausewitz, 1976). The ROC therefore needs strict rules of engagement and constant communication with Washington and Tokyo.

The second risk is abandonment. A future United States administration could decide that SCS claims are not worth supporting. Realism accepts this as a structural reality. Walt notes that alliances form and dissolve based on threat perception, so Taipei must diversify its partners and keep its own indigenous capabilities credible (Walt, 1998, 37).

A constructivist strategy would bet on shared identity with the democratic world and hope that norms around UNCLOS and freedom of navigation will constrain Beijing. That bet is attractive but insufficient. A realist strategy, informed by Clausewitz, accepts that norms follow power, and that Taiwan must first make coercion expensive before it can make cooperation attractive. Our ends are sovereignty, open SLOCs, deterrence, and partnership. Our ways are denial, legal hedging, coast guard presence, economic and informational statecraft, and quiet interoperability. Our means are a larger coast guard, selective economic leverage, and disciplined messaging. Held together, these give ROC a fighting chance in a contested sea.

Strategy, as Yarger argues, is ultimately about "the effective and efficient employment of the instruments of power to achieve policy objectives" under conditions of uncertainty (Yarger, 2006, 5). For the ROC, SCS is not a place to seek glory. It is a place to buy time, preserve options, and raise the cost of aggression. Power must be conserved, politics must command force, and partners must be cultivated before they are needed. If Taipei holds to that line, the SCS can remain what it is today, rather than becoming the opening move in a larger tragedy.

